>
  <h2>Two phases, two kinds of work</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Mitigation</th><th>Reconstruction</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Goal</strong></td><td>Stop the damage from getting worse</td><td>Put the house back the way it was</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Work</strong></td><td>Extraction, tear-out of what can't be saved, drying equipment, cleaning, antimicrobials</td><td>Insulation, drywall, paint, trim, flooring, cabinets, sometimes electrical and plumbing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Timing</strong></td><td>Starts within hours, usually lasts days</td><td>Starts after drying is confirmed, often lasts weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Who does it</strong></td><td>Restoration technicians</td><td>A general contractor and trades</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Licensing</strong></td><td>Often no specific license, varies by place</td><td>Usually a contractor license and permits, depending on your state and city</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Estimate</strong></td><td>Its own estimate or invoice</td><td>A separate repair estimate, often much larger</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Many restoration companies do both. Plenty don't, and pass the rebuild to a partner or leave it to you.</p>

  <h2>Why the drying company wants the rebuild</h2>
  <p>Mitigation is fast, emergency work, and on many water jobs the rebuild is the larger part of the total. The drying crew has an advantage no other contractor has: they're already in your house, they know the damage, and you already trust them enough to let them in at midnight. So the rebuild pitch often comes before the dehumidifiers are switched off.</p>
  <p>That isn't necessarily bad. One company handling both can mean less coordination and a cleaner hand-off. But it's a <strong>separate decision</strong>, and you're entitled to make it separately:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Read your mitigation paperwork</strong> for language about reconstruction, "completion of all repairs," or a cancellation fee if you use someone else. See <a href="/guides/restoration-work-authorization-assignment-of-benefits/">before you sign</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Wait for the insurer's repair estimate</strong> before signing a rebuild contract. It's the baseline every bid should be compared with.</li>
    <li><strong>Get at least one other bid</strong> from a licensed general contractor.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The day-one decisions that set the rebuild bill</h2>
  <p>Every square foot torn out during mitigation is a square foot rebuilt later. That's why the first decisions matter so much.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Remove and replace</th><th>Dry in place</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Drywall</strong></td><td>A "flood cut" above the wet line, commonly at 2 or 4 feet</td><td>Clean-water drywall caught quickly can often be dried through small holes or by removing baseboard</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Cabinets</strong></td><td>Removal, then new cabinets and often new countertops</td><td>Remove the toe-kick and dry underneath, or inject dry air into cavities</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Hardwood floors</strong></td><td>Tear out and replace, often across several rooms to match</td><td>Drying mats that pull moisture up through the boards</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Carpet</strong></td><td>Pad is usually replaced; carpet is often replaced</td><td>Carpet itself can sometimes be dried and cleaned after a clean-water leak</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Insulation</strong></td><td>Soaked insulation is usually removed</td><td>Rarely dried in place</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="notice notice-amber"><strong>The incentive on both sides:</strong> a company that expects the rebuild has a reason to tear out more, because more removal means a larger second job. A company paid only for drying under tight program targets may remove too little, which can leave hidden moisture and mold. Neither is a reason to distrust everyone. It's a reason to ask, before anything is removed: "Can this be dried in place, and if not, why?"</p>
  <p>The answer depends on the <a href="/guides/water-damage-categories/">water category</a>, the material and how long it's been wet. Contaminated water and swollen particleboard usually mean removal. Clean water caught within a day or two often doesn't. Ask for photos and moisture readings of anything removed. They'll matter for the insurance estimate too.</p>

  <h2>The matching problem</h2>
  <p>Replace a few rows of flooring in a kitchen that runs into the dining room and hallway, and the new section may not match. The same goes for cabinets, countertops, tile and siding. Who pays to make the whole area match is one of the most argued points in reconstruction.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Some states' claim-handling rules address matching.</strong> Ohio's rules, for example, call for a reasonably uniform appearance when replacement items can't match in quality, color and size.</li>
    <li><strong>Some policies address it directly,</strong> either way.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask your adjuster in writing</strong> how they'll handle continuous flooring or cabinets that can't be matched, before the rebuild is priced.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Drying in place avoids the matching problem entirely, one more reason to ask the question on day one.</p>

  <h2>Supplements, code upgrades and the hidden-damage surprise</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Supplements are normal.</strong> When walls open up, contractors often find more damage than the first estimate assumed. They submit a supplement to the insurer with photos. Ask that you're copied on each one.</li>
    <li><strong>Code upgrades may be required.</strong> If the rebuild must meet current building codes, such as new electrical requirements, the extra cost may fall under your policy's ordinance or law coverage, which is often limited. Check yours.</li>
    <li><strong>Permits take time.</strong> Ask who pulls them and whether the timeline includes inspections.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Following the money</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Separate payments are common:</strong> one for mitigation, one or more for the rebuild, one for contents.</li>
    <li><strong>Your mortgage lender is often named on dwelling checks.</strong> For larger claims, lenders commonly deposit the funds and release them in stages, sometimes after inspections. Contact the lender's loss draft or insurance claims department early to learn its process.</li>
    <li><strong>Depreciation may be withheld</strong> until repairs are finished. Ask what's needed to release it and by when.</li>
    <li><strong>Pay contractors as work is completed,</strong> not far ahead of it, and get lien waivers from the general contractor and major subcontractors as you pay.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>If you have to move out</h2>
  <p>If the house can't be lived in during repairs, your policy's <strong>additional living expenses</strong> coverage, sometimes called loss of use, usually pays the extra cost of living elsewhere: rent or hotel, extra mileage, extra food costs. Keep receipts, and ask your adjuster about limits and how long it lasts, because a slow rebuild uses it up.</p>

  <h2>A simple way to decide</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Let mitigation finish properly.</strong> Get the final moisture readings. See <a href="/guides/moisture-meters-thermal-cameras-is-it-dry/">is it actually dry</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Get the insurer's repair estimate</strong> and read it room by room. See <a href="/guides/how-restoration-estimates-are-priced/">how estimates are priced</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask the drying company for a rebuild bid</strong> if you like their work, and get at least one more from a licensed general contractor.</li>
    <li><strong>Compare scope, not just totals:</strong> materials, matching, permits, timeline and warranty.</li>
    <li><strong>Check the license</strong> your state or city requires for reconstruction.</li>
  </ol>

